that they really care about.

00:14:59.458 --> 00:15:06.265
And I got to give again a talk on what it
means to integrate grief into your life.

00:15:06.265 --> 00:15:08.489
I lost a parent while I was at the GSB.

00:15:08.489 --> 00:15:13.242
And the experience of dealing with
that and of studying grief and

00:15:13.242 --> 00:15:18.171
its impact on like human psychology,
and just just your life, and

00:15:18.171 --> 00:15:22.747
what we get wrong about it, and
what we get right about it, and

00:15:22.747 --> 00:15:25.937
putting that in the context of a pandemic.

00:15:25.937 --> 00:15:30.694
Just being able to put an academic
structure around something as personal as

00:15:30.694 --> 00:15:33.011
grief was really empowering for me.

00:15:33.011 --> 00:15:37.586
And being able to give a talk on that in
the middle of a pandemic that goes on

00:15:37.586 --> 00:15:40.588
the GSB YouTube was
incredibly powerful also.

00:15:40.588 --> 00:15:44.993
I also encourage everyone here
to look at a nondisclosure.

00:15:44.993 --> 00:15:49.344
It's the GSB magazine you can find it on
medium and it's accessible to everyone and

00:15:49.344 --> 00:15:51.520
I got to work with
really awesome folks and

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:54.962
just writing pieces for
the GSB magazine called nondisclosure.

00:15:54.962 --> 00:15:57.271
So for instance, be advocated for

00:15:57.271 --> 00:16:02.525
making all clubs at the GSB completely
duties free and that was something that I

00:16:02.525 --> 00:16:07.698
got to reply with a coursemate I wrote
something on just body image and

00:16:07.698 --> 00:16:12.815
eating disorders and what that means in
terms of being a professional woman and

00:16:12.815 --> 00:16:17.330
navigating all sorts of body politics and
the role of gender in that.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:20.796
And that was another thing that I
got to do it for the GSB magazine,

00:16:20.796 --> 00:16:25.590
completely unrelated to any three but
related to career but not directly.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:29.576
Yeah, so non disclosure, low key notes,
do awesome things to check out and

00:16:29.576 --> 00:16:31.146
do my favorite experiences.

00:16:31.146 --> 00:16:36.270
Yeah, they're just opportunities every
single day to speak up in classes.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:39.693
And for those of you who are not
familiar with it already,

00:16:39.693 --> 00:16:43.700
the GSB doc is another amazing
experience that I got to partake in.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:47.372
And folks typically do where every
Wednesday night you spend an hour

00:16:47.372 --> 00:16:51.444
listening to two of your classmates
describe their journey through life.

00:16:51.444 --> 00:16:55.727
And that spans all aspects of your life,
not just your career and the experience of

00:16:55.727 --> 00:16:59.454
giving a talk but also listening to
classmates describe their lives and

00:16:59.454 --> 00:17:02.680
their backgrounds every Wednesday night.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:04.340
I'm was incredibly empowering.

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:08.663
And so I think the GSB is a place that
encourages you to bring all of yourself

00:17:08.663 --> 00:17:12.309
into your career and
your social life and your personal life.

00:17:12.309 --> 00:17:15.778
And it really teaches you how to
communicate all the things that

00:17:15.778 --> 00:17:18.800
are important to you and
I really had a blast with that.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.950
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you so much for sharing.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:25.590
And those are all amazing things
to share with our audience today,

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:29.070
as all of you on this call
are learning more about the GSB.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:31.956
And those are programs that,
as Areeba said,

00:17:31.956 --> 00:17:34.629
do encourage you to bring your whole self.

00:17:34.629 --> 00:17:39.478
And also a lot of them are mentioned
whether it's Arbuckle fellows or talk or

00:17:39.478 --> 00:17:43.645
low keynote of just pushing you to
develop yourself as a leader and

00:17:43.645 --> 00:17:47.315
understand who you are and
how you can Influence others.

00:17:47.315 --> 00:17:49.110
So those are all just great examples.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.755
Thank you so much.

00:17:50.755 --> 00:17:54.533
So one of our most highest upvoted
questions is what I'm going to pose

00:17:54.533 --> 00:17:56.130
next to all of you.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:18:00.568
And this is obviously specific to the
theme today of just talking about being

00:18:00.568 --> 00:18:02.521
a woman in business and a leader.

00:18:02.521 --> 00:18:04.580
How do you think Stanford specifically?

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:08.497
And this could be something again,
specific to your time at Stanford or

00:18:08.497 --> 00:18:11.230
even just obtaining an MBA from Stanford?

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:13.986
How has that prepared you as a leader,

00:18:13.986 --> 00:18:17.354
specifically a female
leader in your career?

00:18:17.354 --> 00:18:21.233
So I'm just going to start now
at the top of migrated on zoom.

00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:25.280
[LAUGH] it says no specific order, but
Heather you're at the top of my grid.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.260
So we'll start with you on this one.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:30.680
&gt;&gt; Sure, perfect, female founding
team of a Female Centric Company.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.820
I can definitely talk to this theme.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:40.029
So a couple of things for me,
the first is that there are a few specific

00:18:40.029 --> 00:18:45.207
classes at Stanford that are particularly
focused on female leadership.

00:18:45.207 --> 00:18:47.050
And or women in leadership and

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:50.880
both of them to the to their
specifically women leadership.

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:52.568
They also have diverse leadership and

00:18:52.568 --> 00:18:54.630
other types of classes
that encompass women.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:19:00.161
But there are two that are specifically
focused on women in leadership positions

00:19:00.161 --> 00:19:06.090
and one with Fern Mandelbaum and one
with Laurie Laura, Arriaga and Reessen.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:09.695
They are different quarters, so
you can take both of them and

00:19:09.695 --> 00:19:12.035
they have slightly different events.

00:19:12.035 --> 00:19:15.247
And I actually found those
classes to be to be great,

00:19:15.247 --> 00:19:18.755
because of all the amazing
female speakers that came in.

00:19:18.755 --> 00:19:22.933
I'm great because of like the tactical
tools and exercises that you do.

00:19:22.933 --> 00:19:28.195
But also primarily because it's a lot of
really strong, amazing women in the class

00:19:28.195 --> 00:19:32.726
and this amazing female professor
who really cares about the women in.

00:19:32.726 --> 00:19:35.710
And often you will get a few men and
it's I'm always so

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:39.450
thrilled when men join those
classes just for the record.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:42.566
I think they bring a really
interesting perspective and

00:19:42.566 --> 00:19:47.005
shows that they are interested in learning
about how 50% of the population is

00:19:47.005 --> 00:19:48.520
impacted in their work.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:50.718
I think it's really eye opening.

00:19:50.718 --> 00:19:52.720
So, that was one way.

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:56.791
And then the other one I think was
really I came in coming from a pretty

00:19:56.791 --> 00:19:59.620
male dominated field and consulting.

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:04.464
And I think I battled early in my career
before coming to GSB a lot of this

00:20:04.464 --> 00:20:09.556
stereotypes associated with being
an assertive woman in the workplace,

00:20:09.556 --> 00:20:14.089
especially a young, assertive woman and
late in the workplace.

00:20:14.089 --> 00:20:17.817
And I was really struggling kind
of with my self concept and

00:20:17.817 --> 00:20:21.241
how to stay true to my
personal leadership style, and

00:20:21.241 --> 00:20:24.839
be an authentic leader, but
still be taken seriously.

00:20:24.839 --> 00:20:27.220
And can tell you I'm a highly
emotional person and

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:29.390
I wear my emotions on my sleeve.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:34.030
So I'm kind of trying to figure out
how to balance that in the workplace.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.354
And all of these the opportunities
that I got to kind

00:20:37.354 --> 00:20:39.990
of put my leadership skills to the test.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:43.460
So through Arbuckle,
through interpersonal dynamics,

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:47.011
all of these classes helped me
become a lot more confident.

00:20:47.011 --> 00:20:48.432
And how I was showing up and

00:20:48.432 --> 00:20:52.120
then my specific brand of leadership
how to place in the workplace.

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:54.732
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:20:54.732 --> 00:20:59.202
Anneke I know you already shared with us
in terms of your most memorable experience

00:20:59.202 --> 00:21:02.002
being your time working
with women in management.

00:21:02.002 --> 00:21:03.694
But if you have anything else to add,

00:21:03.694 --> 00:21:07.490
we'd love to hear that in terms of
how it helped you in your career.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for
sure when folks asked me what,

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:12.441
did you actually learn
anything in your MBA?

00:21:12.441 --> 00:21:17.661
I always say that particularly I think
this is unique to the Stanford program,

00:21:17.661 --> 00:21:22.170
my number one learning and
takeaway was that authentically sharing

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:26.934
vulnerability with other people is
the quickest way to build rapport.

00:21:26.934 --> 00:21:33.015
And sort of like the kind of intimacy
that you need to do anything.

00:21:33.015 --> 00:21:36.262
That's have a relationship,
build a company, lead a team,

00:21:36.262 --> 00:21:40.200
all of those things come from
the authentic sharing of vulnerability.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:44.504
Which is wait, what I feel like that
is not aligned with what most people

00:21:44.504 --> 00:21:48.967
think an MBA program is about,
which is about sort of beating your chest.

00:21:48.967 --> 00:21:51.390
And how can you be the next
Titan of industry?

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:55.138
And that was not at all my experience
at Stanford, from everything from

00:21:55.138 --> 00:21:59.140
the curriculum, courses that have
already been described here in detail.

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:03.390
But talk, I think, is a great example,
women groups is a great example.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:07.894
All of the ways that you build connections
with other people, and find ways to

00:22:07.894 --> 00:22:12.829
continue to show up more vulnerably in
an authentic way, really, really helpful.

00:22:12.829 --> 00:22:17.506
And that is something that has allowed
me I think to lean into my own style of,

00:22:17.506 --> 00:22:22.404
sort of leadership as a woman, of bringing
that kind of vulnerability to the way

00:22:22.404 --> 00:22:25.060
that I engage with my
team the way that I am.

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:27.900
Honest with my team,
in what I do and don't know.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:30.560
The way that I'm honest with my
team when giving them feedback,

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.219
the way that I'm honest with my team,
if I messed at a startup and

00:22:33.219 --> 00:22:34.959
things are failing, what do I tell them?

00:22:34.959 --> 00:22:39.300
All of those things require you being able
to bring that sort of vulnerability, and

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:40.860
sort of confidence, right?

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:43.250
I think when you're not
bringing vulnerability,

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:45.259
it's sometimes about putting up errors.

00:22:45.259 --> 00:22:49.096
And there's there's all kinds of negative
externalities that can come from that

00:22:49.096 --> 00:22:52.875
which is, I think aligned with like a more
traditionally kind of maybe male way of

00:22:52.875 --> 00:22:55.755
approaching business and decision making.

00:22:55.755 --> 00:22:59.127
And so, while it is not exclusively about,
sort of being a woman or not,

00:22:59.127 --> 00:23:02.078
I think that's something that's
been really helpful for me.

00:23:02.078 --> 00:23:04.655
And something I've heard from a lot
of my female classmates as well.

00:23:05.805 --> 00:23:07.215
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:23:07.215 --> 00:23:11.257
Tamar, I have you next in my grid,
[LAUGH].

00:23:11.257 --> 00:23:12.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love this question.

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:14.920
I definitely agree with
everything that's been said.

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:16.910
And Heather, I love that you're emotional.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:21.012
I think that something that I learned
from Stanford is that there's power in

00:23:21.012 --> 00:23:21.790
difference.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:25.448
No matter who you are, where you come
from, and to not show that part of

00:23:25.448 --> 00:23:30.010
yourself is actually you're doing
a disservice to yourself into your team.

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:32.975
So, I've always been incredibly bubbly,
but

00:23:32.975 --> 00:23:35.949
also I'm really happy
to ask tough questions.

00:23:35.949 --> 00:23:37.545
And I realized in the first few weeks
at Stanford if there was a professor or

00:23:37.545 --> 00:23:38.627
a guest that said something
that I disagreed with I

00:23:38.627 --> 00:23:39.795
would immediately raise my hand and
ask a question.

00:23:39.795 --> 00:23:46.611
Or say this is why I disagree.

00:23:46.611 --> 00:23:49.386
And it was a little bit scary at first,
but

00:23:49.386 --> 00:23:53.286
I realized that people saw
that as authentic leadership.

00:23:53.286 --> 00:23:54.234
And even if they disagreed,
it was something that we can have

00:23:54.234 --> 00:23:54.780
a conversation about afterward.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:01.626
And it made me much more confident
to do that, not only in class but

00:24:01.626 --> 00:24:03.330
in going forward.

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:07.141
I also think was a really important lesson
for me because even though the team I'm on

00:24:07.141 --> 00:24:09.808
at Netflix has the same name as
the team I was on at YouTube,

00:24:09.808 --> 00:24:11.398
they're completely different.

00:24:11.398 --> 00:24:15.716
I'm working on finance and strategy,
which is something that I didn't actually

00:24:15.716 --> 00:24:18.542
have experience in before
my internship at Netflix.

00:24:18.542 --> 00:24:19.938
I was much more on the content side,

00:24:19.938 --> 00:24:21.809
I majored in literature
when I was at Columbia.

00:24:21.809 --> 00:24:24.950
I felt like I was a soft skill person.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:28.706
And so, to know that there was power in my
difference, to know that if I showed up, I

00:24:28.706 --> 00:24:32.520
look different, I speak different, I came
from Google, I'm going to wear different

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.250
things than people who are on my team who
mostly came from consulting and banking.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.509
That's actually a huge plus for me.

00:24:38.509 --> 00:24:41.790
People are going to remember me because
my name is completely different.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:42.758
It's hard to pronounce.

00:24:42.758 --> 00:24:45.024
I have to tell you again
how to pronounce it.

00:24:45.024 --> 00:24:46.330
That means you remember it.

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:48.670
Yes, Anneke, I see you snuffing.

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:52.840
And then, also, just own that,
show up as that person.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:57.927
And so, I see question about imposter
syndrome here, too, and I feel like that

00:24:57.927 --> 00:25:02.671
goes into being a woman and learning how
to lead through, feeling like maybe I look

00:25:02.671 --> 00:25:07.582
different, or I seem different, or the way
I talk is different than everyone here.

00:25:07.582 --> 00:25:10.568
But, in fact,
I found that it was my greatest strength.

00:25:10.568 --> 00:25:12.018
And the more that I leaned into it,

00:25:12.018 --> 00:25:14.268
the more that I remember what
was said in my classes and

00:25:14.268 --> 00:25:17.480
the better connections that I made with
people in and outside of my classes.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:21.076
So that's absolutely something that
I've brought with me to Netflix.

00:25:21.076 --> 00:25:24.669
And something that definitely makes
me stand out at Netflix, as well.

00:25:24.669 --> 00:25:27.721
And I think that has made
me a much better worker.

00:25:27.721 --> 00:25:31.960
And if I had tried or if I tried to be
a version of me that I am actually not,

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:33.446
I'm going to be unhappy and

00:25:33.446 --> 00:25:38.150
my work is actually going to be worse than
if I'm just the real version of myself.

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:40.903
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:25:40.903 --> 00:25:44.346
Sithara, I have you next,
if you want to answer that.

00:25:44.346 --> 00:25:47.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah,
I love what everyone's been saying.

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:51.421
So let me see if I can say something
that's a little [LAUGH] different since

00:25:51.421 --> 00:25:55.693
it's all such kind of good reflection
on the experience at Stanford, I think.

00:25:55.693 --> 00:26:00.232
I was thinking about this and I was like,
what am I as a female leader?

00:26:00.232 --> 00:26:04.800
And I think I immediately thought
of a class I took at spring

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:08.914
quarter of my second year
called Acting With Power.

00:26:08.914 --> 00:26:13.400
And the class is kind of taught
kind of both with a professor and

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:17.039
with a woman who has more
of an acting background.

00:26:17.039 --> 00:26:21.126
And the thinking and it's so strange
to take an acting class at the GSB and

00:26:21.126 --> 00:26:22.340
it's not like that.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:24.234
I especially had no talent and

00:26:24.234 --> 00:26:28.033
like dreaded getting up in front
of class to act out scenes.

00:26:28.033 --> 00:26:33.449
But the thinking is really, how can you
experiment with different behaviors and

00:26:33.449 --> 00:26:35.361
saying different things and

00:26:35.361 --> 00:26:40.312
inhabiting different characters to
kind of figure out what works for you.

00:26:40.312 --> 00:26:44.032
So, there's something about that, I think,

00:26:44.032 --> 00:26:48.961
post-GSB that's really empowered
me to feel like, listen,

00:26:48.961 --> 00:26:55.815
if something isn't working,
I can try something else, and that's okay.

00:26:55.815 --> 00:27:00.469
I've been in situations, healthcare can
be a really old school industry, and

00:27:00.469 --> 00:27:05.123
I've been in situations where men just
have really overpowering attitudes and

00:27:05.123 --> 00:27:07.981
are kind of talking over
each other in meetings.

00:27:07.981 --> 00:27:12.661
And I feel like I can experiment I can be,
okay, I can try to match that,

00:27:12.661 --> 00:27:14.463
and see if I get a response.

00:27:14.463 --> 00:27:17.300
And if I don't get a response,
I can try something else.

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:23.072
And I feel like that was really empowering
to realize that I could adopt this kind of

00:27:23.072 --> 00:27:26.833
curious mindset of experimentation to
figure out kind of what resonated.

00:27:26.833 --> 00:27:31.439
I think the other part of it is
honestly getting to just meet

00:27:31.439 --> 00:27:36.139
your classmates who are just so
incredible and talented and

00:27:36.139 --> 00:27:39.814
[LAUGH] honestly intimidating in so
many ways.

00:27:39.814 --> 00:27:44.853
And I feel like I've had so many text
messages and conversations with friends,

00:27:44.853 --> 00:27:49.815
post-GSB, about, I've been in this
situation it made me feel a little weird

00:27:49.815 --> 00:27:53.900
or I'm facing this challenge at work,
how would you handle it?

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:57.090
And these are friendships that
are deeply personal friendships, right?

00:27:57.090 --> 00:28:00.339
We go on vacation [LAUGH] together,
we do other things together, but

00:28:00.339 --> 00:28:03.018
it's really lovely to feel
like you can also have really

00:28:03.018 --> 00:28:06.267
deep meaningful conversations
about your career and leadership,

00:28:06.267 --> 00:28:09.530
in addition to all of the other
personal things that you care about.

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:12.550
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:16.169
Araeba, if there's anything else to add,
feel free.

00:28:16.169 --> 00:28:20.934
&gt;&gt; I think we covered most bases but
I will plus one what Sithara just

00:28:20.934 --> 00:28:24.994
said about having those people
who you meet at the GSB,

00:28:24.994 --> 00:28:28.803
who keep you honest,
friends who keep you honest.

00:28:28.803 --> 00:28:34.164
I think as I approach my career and
my role and get used to working

00:28:34.164 --> 00:28:39.426
with a lot of folks who come
from very different backgrounds,

00:28:39.426 --> 00:28:45.320
it's nice to still have those people
in your life who know all of you.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:52.050
Yeah, just folks who you can talk to
about things that are coming up that

00:28:52.050 --> 00:28:55.640
it's not always appropriate
to talk about at work.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:59.481
What I'm thinking about is I will spend
a lot of time working with people

00:28:59.481 --> 00:29:01.540
who don't necessarily look like me.

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:04.720
I work with a lot of white
male engineers every day.

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:08.244
I talk to, maybe,
one woman for every four men.

00:29:08.244 --> 00:29:14.320
[LAUGH] And so, it's really nice to
have my lovely group of mostly female,

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:17.020
GSB besties to text and talk to.

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:19.603
And I just kind of describe
how I'm really feeling.

00:29:19.603 --> 00:29:22.044
Which you don't always have
the luxury of doing that at work.

00:29:22.044 --> 00:29:26.112
And having people who know you
at this deep visceral level, and

00:29:26.112 --> 00:29:31.409
who live within spitting distance of you
in San Francisco, it's such a luxury and

00:29:31.409 --> 00:29:36.586
it allows me to kind of compartmentalize
in other aspects of my career and life.

00:29:36.586 --> 00:29:39.981
I think the other thing
I was going to say is,

00:29:39.981 --> 00:29:44.154
not necessarily related
to just being female, but

00:29:44.154 --> 00:29:49.684
the GSB did teach me that people
are people no matter what industry or

00:29:49.684 --> 00:29:52.324
role or geography they're in.

00:29:52.324 --> 00:29:56.679
I think as an immigrant I already sort of
knew this, at some level that my friends

00:29:56.679 --> 00:29:59.680
back home and
my friends here they have a lot in common.

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:03.028
And a lot, you can build relationships and

00:30:03.028 --> 00:30:08.703
build strong connections with anyone
regardless of their background.

00:30:08.703 --> 00:30:12.450
And I think I'm more cognizant about
approaching folks who are much more

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:16.138
senior than me, or approaching folks
with very different skill sets or

00:30:16.138 --> 00:30:17.316
backgrounds than me.

00:30:17.316 --> 00:30:21.988
And knowing that I can build a connection
with them and I can have rapport with

00:30:21.988 --> 00:30:25.500
them, and influence them,
and have them influence me.

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:29.334
I think that was something that I realized
at the GSB with the help of classes like,

00:30:29.334 --> 00:30:31.559
what is proverbially called Touchy Feely,
but

00:30:31.559 --> 00:30:34.020
it's officially called
Interpersonal Dynamics.

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:37.740
There's a lot of classes on interpersonal
dynamics beyond just Touchy Feely.

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:41.763
But I think just being able to understand
what motivates someone, what moves them,

00:30:41.763 --> 00:30:45.107
how to communicate with them,
how to build a relationship with them,

00:30:45.107 --> 00:30:47.390
regardless of whether they look like me or
not.

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:51.444
That was really empowering to me because,
I think, before the GSB I always sought

00:30:51.444 --> 00:30:55.295
out sponsors and mentors who were also
women or who were also people of color.

00:30:55.295 --> 00:31:00.672
And I am still going to keep doing that,
but just by virtue of the space I work in,

00:31:00.672 --> 00:31:06.071
there aren't that many of those and I'm
going to need everyone in my wheelhouse.

00:31:06.071 --> 00:31:10.160
And so building the confidence to build
relationships with everyone around me,

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:11.974
I think I got a lot of that at the GSB.

00:31:11.974 --> 00:31:13.610
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:16.807
I'm going to ask the next question and I'm
going to just direct it to two of you, but

00:31:16.807 --> 00:31:17.727
by all means, just for

00:31:17.727 --> 00:31:20.790
sake of time so that we can try to get
through as many questions as possible.

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:25.830
But if someone wants to say something,
by all means, speak up.

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:30.374
So, this one is actually I'm going to
start with Tamar because it's directed

00:31:30.374 --> 00:31:34.078
towards a point that you made
about just how you chose the GSB.

00:31:34.078 --> 00:31:38.770
And the question is what was the final
differentiator that made you

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:39.954
choose the GSB?

00:31:39.954 --> 00:31:43.524
And I want to make sure we just focus on
the GSB because none of us then ended up

00:31:43.524 --> 00:31:45.260
attending other universities.

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:47.310
[LAUGH] So
we can't speak specifically to that.

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:51.391
But just, maybe, what was your thought
process as you were making your decision

00:31:51.391 --> 00:31:55.532
and that might help people considering the
GSB as a place to apply but also one day,

00:31:55.532 --> 00:31:56.630
hopefully attempt?

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:59.291
So ask Tamar and then, maybe,
Sithara, you can also, and

00:31:59.291 --> 00:32:01.110
we'll have both of you chime in on this.

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:03.338
Yeah, definitely.

00:32:03.338 --> 00:32:06.950
It was a really difficult decision for
me because I really wanted to be

00:32:06.950 --> 00:32:11.000
international, and I talked a little bit
about the fact that I could have done

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.759
dual degree at the other
school that I was choosing.

00:32:13.759 --> 00:32:18.087
But I think what made me choose
the GSB was that honestly it scared me

00:32:18.087 --> 00:32:22.957
a little bit more, there was this
focus on, interpersonal dynamics, and

00:32:22.957 --> 00:32:24.751
getting deep with people.

00:32:24.751 --> 00:32:28.651
I had lived in the bay for a long time and
had heard of venture capital but

00:32:28.651 --> 00:32:30.780
had no idea what that actually meant.

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:35.486
And so, I felt the GSB would push me out
of my comfort zone in a way that if I went

00:32:35.486 --> 00:32:39.827
to any other school I might not have
to be pushed out of my comfort zone,

00:32:39.827 --> 00:32:41.832
I could really easily retreat.

00:32:41.832 --> 00:32:46.875
And so, I wanted to scare myself a little
bit, and so that's why I chose GSB.

00:32:46.875 --> 00:32:48.378
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:32:48.378 --> 00:32:50.228
Sithara, I'm curious to hear,

00:32:50.228 --> 00:32:53.345
you can answer the same question
why you choose the GSB.

00:32:53.345 --> 00:32:56.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's actually it's interesting,
it's a little similar.

00:32:56.110 --> 00:33:01.095
When I was trying to decide,
I went to weekend at the GSB,

00:33:01.095 --> 00:33:03.915
and to be honest, I didn't love it.

00:33:03.915 --> 00:33:08.685
I didn't walk away being, my gosh, this
place is amazing, I'm in love with it,

00:33:08.685 --> 00:33:09.707
I have to go here.

00:33:09.707 --> 00:33:12.809
I walked away and maybe I'm too
much of a total Midwesterner,

00:33:12.809 --> 00:33:16.508
where I don't like talking about my
feelings with people I've just met,

00:33:16.508 --> 00:33:18.792
I'm like,
why are you asking me about this?

00:33:18.792 --> 00:33:21.894
We need to chit chat about the weather for

00:33:21.894 --> 00:33:25.188
a couple of months
before we can get there.

00:33:25.188 --> 00:33:29.158
And I feel there's so
much emphasis on the, of course,

00:33:29.158 --> 00:33:32.886
everyone at the GSB says
the touchy feely part of it.

00:33:32.886 --> 00:33:37.681
And then kind of intangibles, and
I feel, especially, coming from

00:33:37.681 --> 00:33:42.983
a nonprofit background, I feel like I
want to learn about business, right?

00:33:42.983 --> 00:33:45.641
I care about the academics.

00:33:45.641 --> 00:33:50.756
And then I thought about it and I was,
you literally wrote your GSB essay

00:33:50.756 --> 00:33:55.629
about taking on uncomfortable
experiences and seeking those out.

00:33:55.629 --> 00:34:00.187
So, isn't it interesting that this
makes you feel uncomfortable, and

00:34:00.187 --> 00:34:03.486
isn't that a sign that
there's a lot to learn here?

00:34:03.486 --> 00:34:07.858
So, that's actually kind of what
ended up making the decision.

00:34:07.858 --> 00:34:11.101
And I have to say,
I walked into day one of the GSB being,

00:34:11.101 --> 00:34:14.413
I don't know if I'm going to this,
but I'll learn a lot and

00:34:14.413 --> 00:34:17.393
then I'll have it on my resume,
and that's fine.

00:34:17.393 --> 00:34:21.172
And now I kind of laugh at how I think
about it because it ended up being

00:34:21.172 --> 00:34:24.303
such a rewarding experience and
so much more than that.

00:34:24.303 --> 00:34:27.491
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you so much for-
&gt;&gt; I'd love to just add a really quick

00:34:27.491 --> 00:34:28.342
other reason.

00:34:28.342 --> 00:34:31.661
For me,
a lot of it to do with program size.

00:34:31.661 --> 00:34:35.585
So I came from an undergraduate
experience, it was very small,

00:34:35.585 --> 00:34:38.429
was all residential on campus,
I loved that.

00:34:38.429 --> 00:34:43.345
And the other MBA programme I was looking
at had maybe 1000 or more students.

00:34:43.345 --> 00:34:47.042
And the size of the GSB programme was
really attractive to me because you have

00:34:47.042 --> 00:34:49.898
an opportunity to really get
to know all your classmates.

00:34:49.898 --> 00:34:54.349
I know literally all of my classmates, and
that is really pays dividends and being

00:34:54.349 --> 00:34:58.813
able to build relationships and having
a network that is really, really strong.

00:34:58.813 --> 00:35:01.876
And then also just have more of
an opportunity to meet professors,

00:35:01.876 --> 00:35:02.976
meet administrators.

00:35:02.976 --> 00:35:05.815
There's just sort of fewer
people up in the mix, and so

00:35:05.815 --> 00:35:07.682
you just have more opportunities.

00:35:07.682 --> 00:35:11.485
&gt;&gt; That's a great point, and if there's
anything else anyone else wants to add,

00:35:11.485 --> 00:35:12.412
please feel free.

00:35:12.412 --> 00:35:15.279
I do want to call out, so
far I've mentioned admin weekend, and

00:35:15.279 --> 00:35:18.375
those of you on the call today
are thinking about applying, right?

00:35:18.375 --> 00:35:21.475
We have a September deadline, we have
a January deadline for round one and

00:35:21.475 --> 00:35:21.975
round two.

00:35:21.975 --> 00:35:24.642
Both of those rounds do
have an admin weekend.

00:35:24.642 --> 00:35:29.564
So if you apply in those rounds and you
are accepted, I mean, you have the chance

00:35:29.564 --> 00:35:34.047
then to, hopefully, by the time you
are admitted it might be in person,

00:35:34.047 --> 00:35:37.010
it might be hybrid,
some of it might be online.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:41.970
But you will have a chance to really
get to know students, and alumni, and

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:45.738
faculty, and get a sense of
what life is like at the GSB.

00:35:45.738 --> 00:35:47.593
And that is specific to rounds one and
round two.

00:35:47.593 --> 00:35:51.619
So as you're thinking through
what rounds to apply in,

00:35:51.619 --> 00:35:54.453
that's just something to be aware of.

00:35:54.453 --> 00:35:56.528
The next most up voted question is,

00:35:56.528 --> 00:36:00.276
how did your time at Stanford
prepare you to switch industries?

00:36:00.276 --> 00:36:03.861
And as I look at this slide that
I'm sharing with all of you,

00:36:03.861 --> 00:36:07.018
I think almost all of you
did switch industries, and

00:36:07.018 --> 00:36:11.435
even Tamer who might be in the same
technology and media switched roles.

00:36:11.435 --> 00:36:14.210
So that's just really interesting
to highlight in general.

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:18.674
That's not always the case, some people do
returned to the same industry that they

00:36:18.674 --> 00:36:22.580
were in after business school but
also many do use it as a chance to pivot.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:28.763
So, I will choose Anneke and Areeba,
maybe to start answering this one.

00:36:28.763 --> 00:36:31.890
I know Heather you already shared
your kind of your journey into

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:32.952
entrepreneurship.

00:36:32.952 --> 00:36:35.778
So,
&gt;&gt; Yeah, entrepreneurship,

00:36:35.778 --> 00:36:40.476
particularly in the technology
space was very interesting to me.

00:36:40.476 --> 00:36:44.436
So I truly don't think you can find
a better program than Stanford's,

00:36:44.436 --> 00:36:46.091
if that's what you want to do.

00:36:46.091 --> 00:36:50.978
You are literally there in Silicon Valley,
the access to understanding

00:36:50.978 --> 00:36:56.278
the funding ecosystem for startups,
the ecosystem for finding co founders.

00:36:56.278 --> 00:36:58.651
And then all of the skill
sets that you need,

00:36:58.651 --> 00:37:02.191
which are not necessarily the ones
you think you're going to need.

00:37:02.191 --> 00:37:05.111
I found the curriculum was a really,
really good fit for that.

00:37:05.111 --> 00:37:08.628
And it was something that I specifically
asked when I was talking to other

00:37:08.628 --> 00:37:09.212
programs.

00:37:09.212 --> 00:37:14.003
And several other programs literally their
line was we're just as good as Stanford at

00:37:14.003 --> 00:37:16.142
helping you with entrepreneurship.

00:37:16.142 --> 00:37:19.755
Which just was a clear sign to me that
Stanford was the leader in this category,

00:37:19.755 --> 00:37:21.520
and that was very much my experience.

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:25.803
I think very similar to the path that
Heather went through of just being

00:37:25.803 --> 00:37:29.434
able to have access to different
programs and curriculum,

00:37:29.434 --> 00:37:33.960
and just being sort of in the soup of
startup world and entrepreneurship.

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:36.732
I think also just this
line about change lives,

00:37:36.732 --> 00:37:39.308
change organizations, change the world.

00:37:39.308 --> 00:37:44.150
Sounds a little cheesy sometimes, but
it's really true, I felt that in probably

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:47.798
my last week or two on campus when
we had different speakers and

00:37:47.798 --> 00:37:51.755
events that were sort of putting
an end cap on our time at Stanford.

00:37:51.755 --> 00:37:57.438
And it really stood out how much of
the experience was about go forth and

00:37:57.438 --> 00:37:59.087
create something.

00:37:59.087 --> 00:38:01.884
And that doesn't necessarily
have to be entrepreneurship,

00:38:01.884 --> 00:38:03.237
you can do that in other ways.

00:38:03.237 --> 00:38:06.483
But this idea of having
not only the skills,

00:38:06.483 --> 00:38:10.081
but also the little fire
inside yourself to go and

00:38:10.081 --> 00:38:14.821
make something in this world was so
much about that experience,

00:38:14.821 --> 00:38:18.887
and I think Stanford is just
very unique on that front.

00:38:18.887 --> 00:38:20.049
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:38:20.049 --> 00:38:22.155
And, Areeba, if you want to share,

00:38:22.155 --> 00:38:26.585
you already highlighted a little bit
about just how the GSB was integral and

00:38:26.585 --> 00:38:31.662
you kind of heading to Apple, but in terms
of switching industries, how did it help?

00:38:31.662 --> 00:38:36.796
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I will paraphrase this with,
I am an international student and

00:38:36.796 --> 00:38:40.625
for those who are on the call and
are international,

00:38:40.625 --> 00:38:44.986
you know that it's just an added
kind of stressor because.

00:38:44.986 --> 00:38:48.378
I like to joke that I'm only ever in the
States because someone wants me to go to

00:38:48.378 --> 00:38:51.003
their school or someone wants
me to work for their company.

00:38:51.003 --> 00:38:55.681
And I in my head very much see the States
is where I've grown up, I'm 29,

00:38:55.681 --> 00:38:57.781
I've been here since I was 19.

00:38:57.781 --> 00:39:04.564
But definitely, I'm in a visa, and so,
for me going into the GSB, I think,

00:39:04.564 --> 00:39:10.899
a lot of people take time to settle in and
take a few months and get to know people.

00:39:10.899 --> 00:39:14.525
For me, personally,
I support my family and needed a visa, and

00:39:14.525 --> 00:39:17.616
it started with thinking
about career from, I think,

00:39:17.616 --> 00:39:20.592
before I came into the GSB and
had a long to-do list.

00:39:20.592 --> 00:39:25.395
And I think what was helpful for
me and in getting through that list of

00:39:25.395 --> 00:39:30.554
stressful things to-do was the classmates
who were already at the GSB.

00:39:30.554 --> 00:39:35.791
And so, technically, you don't start
doing resume drops and career events and

00:39:35.791 --> 00:39:40.798
whatnot until November, but I think even
just in the summer before the GSB and

00:39:40.798 --> 00:39:42.974
August, September, October.

00:39:42.974 --> 00:39:47.767
I reached out to folks in my class who
were working in product rolls across

00:39:47.767 --> 00:39:51.632
small, big,
medium companies that I was interested in.

00:39:51.632 --> 00:39:54.976
I was mostly looking at product and
big tech and FinTech.

00:39:54.976 --> 00:39:59.304
And instead of just going directly to
potential employers or going to alumni, I

00:39:59.304 --> 00:40:03.632
just reached out to people in my class and
the class above me, and that was really

00:40:03.632 --> 00:40:08.494
helpful because it was Less stressful and
I was trying to build rapport.

00:40:08.494 --> 00:40:11.292
Who am I talking to?

00:40:11.292 --> 00:40:12.801
Who kind of reminds me of me?

00:40:12.801 --> 00:40:14.078
Who would I emulate?

00:40:14.078 --> 00:40:15.700
What company do they work at?

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:19.059
I feel like, I'd like working there,
because I like them, and

00:40:19.059 --> 00:40:20.060
I sound like them.

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:22.150
Where should I apply,
and who do I talk to?

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:24.942
What are some friendly
faces at these places,

00:40:24.942 --> 00:40:27.720
who are not just the resume drop order?

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:30.540
And then what can I say I have to offer,
what's my narrative?

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:31.322
What's my pitch?

00:40:31.322 --> 00:40:34.559
And being able to really refine that
with classmates was super valuable.

00:40:34.559 --> 00:40:39.300
And when it came time to actually go to
networking events, and do resume drops and

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:43.222
do interviews, just having all
that practice was super helpful.

00:40:43.222 --> 00:40:46.825
Not to say everyone should start thinking
about their careers as soon as they get

00:40:46.825 --> 00:40:47.912
into business school.

00:40:47.912 --> 00:40:50.565
Take the summer off,
take some time off, but for

00:40:50.565 --> 00:40:54.590
the international students on this call,
I hear you, it's stressful.

00:40:54.590 --> 00:40:59.560
You have to kind of, you've added stress
and I think just leveraging the classmates

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:04.255
that you have even just at the GSB even,
outside of the amazing alumni network.

00:41:04.255 --> 00:41:06.905
The folks that you're surrounded
with in the classroom and

00:41:06.905 --> 00:41:09.030
that you're about you
are a big place to start.

00:41:10.090 --> 00:41:11.866
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you for sharing.

00:41:11.866 --> 00:41:14.285
The next question I might
pose to all of you, but

00:41:14.285 --> 00:41:17.810
we'll start with Heather since she
didn't answer for the last one.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:21.615
But could you just share a favorite moment
you've seen of women supporting women?

00:41:21.615 --> 00:41:26.598
It could be during your time at the GSB or
your time after, also.

00:41:26.598 --> 00:41:27.520
&gt;&gt; Gosh, yeah.

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:28.791
And I'll share two quickly.

00:41:28.791 --> 00:41:32.083
One is that I have a really close
group of girlfriends from the GSB.

00:41:32.083 --> 00:41:36.567
We have a very active group message
that probably people write in

00:41:36.567 --> 00:41:40.270
it every single day, and
we Zoom every other week.

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:45.487
And we've been there for each other
through pregnancies and jobs switches,

00:41:45.487 --> 00:41:49.750
promotions, parents getting ill,
all kinds of life events.

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.691
And so, I think, just like people
have said, having this really small,

00:41:53.691 --> 00:41:57.316
close set of friends that you feel
you know everything about them and

00:41:57.316 --> 00:41:59.114
they know everything about you.

00:41:59.114 --> 00:42:01.548
That support really continues on.

00:42:01.548 --> 00:42:04.802
The second is a fun note and
it's going to sound, Tamar you feature so

00:42:04.802 --> 00:42:06.360
heavily in my life.

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:09.798
So we when we did our
first photo shoot for

00:42:09.798 --> 00:42:14.460
Frankly we use a bunch of
models from our class at GSB.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:18.853
So, Tamar was one of the models for
Frankly's first ever photo shoot.

00:42:18.853 --> 00:42:22.581
And the same thing,
when we ran a kick starter last fall,

00:42:22.581 --> 00:42:25.543
that was kind of how we
started the business.

00:42:25.543 --> 00:42:28.390
And the women's channel
on our Slack blew up,

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:32.339
people were following it all day,
we got so many text messages.

00:42:32.339 --> 00:42:36.146
So, I think the women in our
class really came together and

00:42:36.146 --> 00:42:41.209
supported [LAUGH] what Jane and I were
trying to do, and are still trying to do.

00:42:41.209 --> 00:42:44.850
So, that has been really fun and
uplifting.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.620
It was people that I knew really well and
that I loved, but

00:42:47.620 --> 00:42:51.025
also people that I didn't know,
as well, from school who came out and

00:42:51.025 --> 00:42:55.005
were like this is what I'm thinking of,
or, I just had a baby and now I'm thinking

00:42:55.005 --> 00:42:58.499
about how I can breastfeed and
not wearing a bra would be great for that.

00:42:58.499 --> 00:43:02.603
So people have been so
helpful in directing ideas our way and

00:43:02.603 --> 00:43:05.486
just supporting us in every way they can.

00:43:05.486 --> 00:43:09.391
Tagging us in comments when Mina Harris
posts about not wearing a bra,

00:43:09.391 --> 00:43:10.790
all kinds of stuff, so.

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:13.340
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:16.860
Tamar, do you want to add
anything to that, specifically?

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:22.439
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's definitely the first
moment that I thought of, Heather,

00:43:22.439 --> 00:43:23.597
because that was so fun.

00:43:23.597 --> 00:43:28.237
A bunch of GSB girls got together in LA
and Heather and Jane were so professional,

00:43:28.237 --> 00:43:28.928
I must say.

00:43:28.928 --> 00:43:31.750
There was a makeup artist,
there was a professional photographer.

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:32.340
It was so fun.

00:43:33.490 --> 00:43:37.451
Another moment that I have is,
I took this class called Paths to Power,

00:43:37.451 --> 00:43:40.303
which is a little bit
similar to Acting with Power.

00:43:40.303 --> 00:43:43.203
There's lots of classes about
power at Stanford, which are fun.

00:43:43.203 --> 00:43:45.096
But this is all about, I think like,

00:43:45.096 --> 00:43:48.531
engaging in power in a way that
a lot of people find uncomfortable.

00:43:48.531 --> 00:43:50.286
But learning what the tactics are so

00:43:50.286 --> 00:43:54.380
that you can understand how you can do it
yourself, but also how people are engaging

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:57.442
with power around you in a way
that you might not have noticed.

00:43:57.442 --> 00:44:02.428
And, afterward, my friend Marta, and I,
and my class, created sort of like a group

00:44:02.428 --> 00:44:05.686
of women who would try to do
powerful things together for

00:44:05.686 --> 00:44:08.896
the spring quarter,
the class happens in the winter.

00:44:08.896 --> 00:44:10.287
And we called it Power Posse.

00:44:10.287 --> 00:44:11.710
And it was actually so fun and

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:14.330
we still have our WhatsApp
group to help each other.

00:44:14.330 --> 00:44:17.950
And I think it's like, okay,
I want to write a medium post.

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:18.589
Should I post it?

00:44:18.589 --> 00:44:21.809
And we're all sort of like, rah,
rah, you go, you should do this.

00:44:21.809 --> 00:44:25.000
And so, I think that's one way that I'm so

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.087
engaged with women supporting women,
after the GSB.

00:44:29.087 --> 00:44:29.850
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:44:30.900 --> 00:44:33.100
Anneke, do you want to add to that?

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:36.596
&gt;&gt; Yeah, just both Heather and
Tamar referenced Slack channels,

00:44:36.596 --> 00:44:39.386
WhatsApp groups, and
I have to just plus one that.

00:44:39.386 --> 00:44:43.468
I think just to have powerful
amazing women on speed dial,

00:44:43.468 --> 00:44:46.048
in my pocket that I can text them, and

00:44:46.048 --> 00:44:51.826
know that I have someone who can help me
prepare to negotiate an elegant M&amp;A deal.

00:44:51.826 --> 00:44:56.806
Or help me debug my SQL query or advise me
on how to have a difficult conversation

00:44:56.806 --> 00:45:00.060
at work, or
have a gynecologist recommendation.

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:03.500
All of those things,
I can get from the same people.

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:07.691
And it's just like, when people talk about
the network, to have a network of also

00:45:07.691 --> 00:45:11.273
really powerful women,
just is like an extra awesome thing.

00:45:11.273 --> 00:45:12.794
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:45:12.794 --> 00:45:14.908
Sithara, anything else to add to that,

00:45:14.908 --> 00:45:17.530
a time when you've seen
women supporting women?

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:21.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm 100% on the WhatsApp threads.

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:24.089
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I literally was just texting women's

00:45:24.089 --> 00:45:28.976
GSB throughout a question earlier today,
so, it's a real thing.

00:45:28.976 --> 00:45:33.328
I just think about like a month or two
ago, I was like having a hard time at work

00:45:33.328 --> 00:45:37.476
and was meeting a friend of mine just
to get a manicure in San Francisco and

00:45:37.476 --> 00:45:38.637
was talking to her.

00:45:38.637 --> 00:45:43.379
And just ended up just like, totally
surprised me just like crying while we're

00:45:43.379 --> 00:45:45.730
getting a manicure in this nail salon.

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:50.491
And my friend who was my GSB roommate,
who kind of runs her own company and

00:45:50.491 --> 00:45:53.771
who I really kind of look up to,
professionally,

00:45:53.771 --> 00:45:58.091
and think is a total badass,
just was like, it's okay Sithara.

00:45:58.091 --> 00:46:00.854
She was like,
whenever I cry on a Zoom call or

00:46:00.854 --> 00:46:05.640
something like that over the last year,
I've just been telling myself that

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:09.111
it's good to have an executive
cry every now and then.

00:46:09.111 --> 00:46:12.922
[LAUGH] And
it was just such a funny moment of,

00:46:12.922 --> 00:46:18.394
it had never occurred to me that,
one, she would even do that.

00:46:18.394 --> 00:46:22.310
And it was just such a good moment
to be like, I feel so seen and

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:26.466
I feel okay just like sobbing in
a nail salon right now and it's fine.

00:46:26.466 --> 00:46:32.033
[LAUGH] So,
just one moment of kind of many like that.

00:46:32.033 --> 00:46:33.658
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; That's authentic vulnerability,

00:46:33.658 --> 00:46:34.970
sharing that vulnerability.

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:36.920
It's actually really great.

00:46:36.920 --> 00:46:38.881
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you for sharing that.

00:46:38.881 --> 00:46:41.040
Areeba, anything else to add time and

00:46:41.040 --> 00:46:43.944
women were supporting women
that you want to share?

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:49.047
&gt;&gt; I think you guys
covered a lot of bases.

00:46:49.047 --> 00:46:52.089
I don't have anything super substantive.

00:46:52.089 --> 00:46:53.920
So I'll give you that time back.

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:56.380
&gt;&gt; Great, so, another upvoted question.

00:46:57.620 --> 00:46:58.984
So, from your experiences,

00:46:58.984 --> 00:47:02.610
would you say the culture at Stanford
is more collaborative or competitive?

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:07.200
I think I know what you guys might answer,
but, it'd be great to

00:47:07.200 --> 00:47:12.139
maybe share an example based on what
your answer is to that question.

00:47:12.139 --> 00:47:13.017
So, Areeba,

00:47:13.017 --> 00:47:17.571
why don't we go the other way now since
you didn't answer that other one?

00:47:17.571 --> 00:47:22.118
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there is zero doubt in my
mind about the answer to this one.

00:47:22.118 --> 00:47:27.190
It is 100% collaborative.

00:47:27.190 --> 00:47:32.125
I was talking to someone the other day,
a mentor outside of the GSB who

00:47:32.125 --> 00:47:37.148
said that he was struck by how I
thought about progress not as zero sum,

00:47:37.148 --> 00:47:42.280
but as something that happens
concurrently for many people, at once.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:46.145
And I was like, yeah,
I got that at the GSB.

00:47:46.145 --> 00:47:50.215
Everyone is just so
willing to help everyone else, and

00:47:50.215 --> 00:47:54.295
it's very much kind of
an attitude of your class.

00:47:54.295 --> 00:47:59.340
I think it's an example, I think just,
if you're organizing an event, for

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:04.233
instance, I headed the deck club but
I was at the GSB with five other amazing

00:48:04.233 --> 00:48:09.259
classmates and Every time we bring
an awesome speaker to campus,

00:48:09.259 --> 00:48:13.461
for instance, if you're bringing
the head of the Senior PM for

00:48:13.461 --> 00:48:18.396
iPhone to campus, like Astec Club,
you would reach out to product club.

00:48:18.396 --> 00:48:22.983
And also, I don't know, like design club
and try to get them to come together so

00:48:22.983 --> 00:48:26.475
that you can get as many classmates
as possible to attend this

00:48:26.475 --> 00:48:27.792
awesome discussion.

00:48:27.792 --> 00:48:31.335
There is no competition for
tech club to do an event and

00:48:31.335 --> 00:48:36.164
eke out another club, it would truly
not even occur to anyone to do that.

00:48:36.164 --> 00:48:40.523
So anytime you're doing something that
might be interesting to another classmate

00:48:40.523 --> 00:48:43.402
or another organization on campus,
you move them in.

00:48:43.402 --> 00:48:48.393
I think it's very similar from
a career point of view, I've never

00:48:48.393 --> 00:48:53.841
really had a classmate who I reached
out to, and not respond to an email.

00:48:53.841 --> 00:48:58.100
So even when you're in MBA 1, MBA 2s,
I think right at the beginning, feel and

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:01.108
accessible, and
just seem to have all of the wisdom, and

00:49:01.108 --> 00:49:04.259
you're trying to figure it out,
like how they got so cool.

00:49:04.259 --> 00:49:08.103
And I think a lot of reservations
reaching out to people and saying, hey,

00:49:08.103 --> 00:49:12.640
I'm an MBA 1 and that overeager person
who's already thinking about interviewing.

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:16.063
Could you please talk to me
about your cool internship, and

00:49:16.063 --> 00:49:20.247
everyone always responds even if they
don't have time to meet with you.

00:49:20.247 --> 00:49:24.589
They're super busy, they're not in the
country, they'll support you by email or

00:49:24.589 --> 00:49:25.821
Slack or WebEx or Zoom.

00:49:25.821 --> 00:49:28.180
Yeah, people are super,
super collaborative.

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:30.661
I think another example
is just interview prep.

00:49:30.661 --> 00:49:35.177
Even if you're applying for the same jobs,
because a lot of people apply to Google

00:49:35.177 --> 00:49:39.243
and Apple and the bigger companies,
I prepped for interviews together.

00:49:39.243 --> 00:49:42.000
I let my classmates know, hey,
I moved to the next round, and

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:45.805
this is someone I talked to who was super
helpful, do you also want to talk to them?

00:49:45.805 --> 00:49:50.579
You make introductions for books at your
old company or the company you enjoy that.

00:49:50.579 --> 00:49:54.397
It's just very much kind of
in the DNA of the school.

00:49:54.397 --> 00:49:57.833
And whether you go to the GSB or not,
I would just advocate for all of us as

00:49:57.833 --> 00:50:01.927
women, just seeing progress is something
that happens concurrently for all of us.

00:50:01.927 --> 00:50:06.322
And I think sometimes, especially in
places where there aren't enough women in

00:50:06.322 --> 00:50:10.908
leadership, there can be the attitude of
I want to be the only woman in power, and

00:50:10.908 --> 00:50:12.866
I didn't see any of that at the GSB.

00:50:12.866 --> 00:50:18.692
And hopefully there will be none of
that on this panel of 108 people either.

00:50:18.692 --> 00:50:19.324
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thank you.

00:50:19.324 --> 00:50:22.111
I don't think we have time to get to
everyone because we just have a few

00:50:22.111 --> 00:50:22.746
minutes left.

00:50:22.746 --> 00:50:27.243
But I'd love to ask Anneke, just going in
reverse order again, to chime in if you

00:50:27.243 --> 00:50:31.094
have anything else to add about
collaborative versus competitive?

00:50:31.094 --> 00:50:33.843
&gt;&gt; I think that point on competing for
jobs was a good one.

00:50:33.843 --> 00:50:36.782
And it made me think of a couple
of real concrete examples.

00:50:36.782 --> 00:50:40.580
Something I see a lot on threads
with GSBers is people who have

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:45.572
the job position for their boss is open,
and they'll blast it out to GSB folks or

00:50:45.572 --> 00:50:49.817
pick specific ones and say, hey,
I'd love for you to interview for

00:50:49.817 --> 00:50:51.789
this job to come and be my boss.

00:50:52.960 --> 00:50:55.793
Or in places where, I've definitely
had friends who went through

00:50:55.793 --> 00:50:59.233
an interview process, didn't get the job,
but then reached out to me and said,

00:50:59.233 --> 00:51:02.541
I think you might be better for this job,
here's all the information for it.

00:51:02.541 --> 00:51:05.453
So that's sort of job sharing, and
trying to help lift people up,

00:51:05.453 --> 00:51:07.270
you see all the time.

00:51:07.270 --> 00:51:11.675
&gt;&gt; Awesome, is there anything else
that any of you would like to add in

00:51:11.675 --> 00:51:14.664
terms of just talking about the culture,
and

00:51:14.664 --> 00:51:19.792
a time maybe that the collaborative
nature of the program was notable to you?

00:51:19.792 --> 00:51:22.897
&gt;&gt; And
I just want to say something really fast.

00:51:22.897 --> 00:51:25.351
And I think that the nature
of the program is so

00:51:25.351 --> 00:51:29.549
collaborative that you will serve yourself
the best if you come to Stanford by

00:51:29.549 --> 00:51:32.455
telling everybody what
you're interested in or what

00:51:32.455 --> 00:51:36.820
you're passionate about because people
will bring that back to you tenfold.

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:40.260
If they know what you care about,
anytime there's an opportunity or

00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:44.352
something that even marginally relates to
it, or if they're thinking about it or

00:51:44.352 --> 00:51:47.463
talking to someone,
they will point that person back to you.

00:51:47.463 --> 00:51:50.361
And that has been one of the most
powerful things that I got out of

00:51:50.361 --> 00:51:51.463
the Stanford network.

00:51:51.463 --> 00:51:55.890
It's just people's understanding
of what I cared about and

00:51:55.890 --> 00:52:01.115
willingness to connect me to the things
that would make sense for that.

00:52:01.115 --> 00:52:02.542
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:52:02.542 --> 00:52:06.230
I'm actually going to flip to the next
slide, and I feel like that's a really

00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:09.637
nice note to end on because you talked
about kind of bringing yourself and

00:52:09.637 --> 00:52:12.969
knowing your interests and
being willing to share that with people.

00:52:12.969 --> 00:52:17.103
And I think as you start to hone
in on that as you go through our

00:52:17.103 --> 00:52:18.776
application process.

00:52:18.776 --> 00:52:22.341
And we ask some big questions,
we're still asking the same question

00:52:22.341 --> 00:52:26.660
that all of our panelists had to answer,
what matters most to you and why Stanford?

00:52:26.660 --> 00:52:30.983
So I encourage all of you to continue
your journey online with us.

00:52:30.983 --> 00:52:34.730
We have a lot of resources up there in
addition to being able to track your

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:38.114
application, you can also learn
more about courses and clubs.

00:52:38.114 --> 00:52:43.414
There are other events offered in the next
few weeks prior to the round one deadline.

00:52:43.414 --> 00:52:46.710
So I also encourage you to check our event
page if you're interested in attending

00:52:46.710 --> 00:52:48.027
another panel such as this one.

00:52:48.027 --> 00:52:50.432
We have regular information sessions, and

00:52:50.432 --> 00:52:53.032
we do have some just in
time webinars coming up.

00:52:53.032 --> 00:52:57.183
We call those just in time, meaning, they
are right before the round one deadline.

00:52:57.183 --> 00:53:00.323
So if you are stuck on something in
your application and need advice or

00:53:00.323 --> 00:53:03.965
guidance from the admissions team and
we are here for you through that process.

00:53:03.965 --> 00:53:05.237
So I encourage you to sign up for those.

00:53:05.237 --> 00:53:08.176
Also, I want to thank our panelists.

00:53:08.176 --> 00:53:10.551
Thank you, all of you for
joining us today.

00:53:10.551 --> 00:53:11.713
Thank you for your time.

00:53:11.713 --> 00:53:15.421
I know many of you are calling in
from work, we just put up a poll, so

00:53:15.421 --> 00:53:18.092
please let us know your
feedback on this event.

00:53:18.092 --> 00:53:21.362
And again, I know everyone is
calling in from all over the world.

00:53:21.362 --> 00:53:25.989
So I hope that everyone has a good
rest of their day, afternoon, morning,

00:53:25.989 --> 00:53:28.861
evening, whatever it is, wherever you are.

00:53:28.861 --> 00:53:34.367
Thank you so much again for
your time today to everyone.

00:53:34.367 --> 00:53:36.544
[MUSIC]

